# Security headers for noahjefferson.pages.dev — Doctrine §16.6
#
# These four cost nothing and break nothing on a static link page.
#
# There is deliberately NO Content-Security-Policy here yet, and that absence is
# honest rather than an oversight: index.html carries its content model as an
# inline <script> (the block labelled "EDIT HERE"), so a strict script-src 'self'
# would reduce the site to its <noscript> fallback. A real CSP needs that script
# and the inline <style> extracted to files first. Hash-pinning instead would
# force regenerating the hash on every content edit, which is the wrong trade
# when editing that file IS the workflow.
#
# No Strict-Transport-Security either: pages.dev is HTTPS-only and Cloudflare
# handles TLS for it. Adding a header with lock-in semantics that cannot be
# tested from here would be worse than leaving it to the platform.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

# CACHE POLICY FOR THE PAGES — and what it does NOT fix, which is the honest half.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-03 against the live site, before writing this: every path
# already returns `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` from the origin — the
# pages, the icons, the stylesheet, all of it, because that is Cloudflare Pages'
# default. `/accessibility` came back with no `cf-cache-status` at all, so a live
# page here is not being served from an edge cache in the first place.
#
# WHAT PROMPTED IT. Two pages were deleted and both kept serving from
# Cloudflare's edge for days afterwards, under `s-maxage=604800` — a directive
# the origin never sent. Those entries are edge-side and pre-date the removals,
# and a `*.pages.dev` hostname is not a zone in the account, so there is nothing
# here that can purge or shorten them. THIS BLOCK DOES NOT FIX THAT. It cannot.
#
# What it does is say the intent out loud: `s-maxage=0` is the one directive
# beyond the platform default, and it addresses shared caches specifically rather
# than relying on `max-age=0` being read that way. If a page is deleted, the next
# request revalidates and the stale copy dies with it.
#
# HTML ONLY, ROUTE BY ROUTE. `/*` would sweep the icons in with it, and a
# blanket rule is how a site loses asset caching it might later want. The `.html`
# forms need no entry — Pages 308s them to the clean URL (verified). A new page
# joins this list in the SAME commit that creates it, and `a11y-gate.mjs`
# refuses a commit where it has not: the list of deployed pages lives there
# already, and a second copy of that list is the drift this family keeps writing
# lessons about.

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=0, must-revalidate

/accessibility
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, s-maxage=0, must-revalidate
